<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swindon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Swindon is a large town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Nearby Wroughton is the probable site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a> in 825, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht of Wessex</a> defeated Mercia. A small market town until the nineteenth century, Swindon grew rapidly after the Great Western Railway built its main locomotive and carriage works there in the 1840s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swindon.jpg" alt="Swindon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Brown · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_David_Murray_John_tower_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5742836.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Swindon lies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, 84 miles (135 km) west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and 36 miles (57 km) east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, on the M4 corridor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> lie just to the north and the North Wessex Downs just to the south. The ancient Ridgeway, described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s oldest road, runs a few miles to the south, with Avebury, the largest megalithic stone circle in the world, and the Uffington White Horse nearby. The town stands at the junction of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a>, and it developed into a transport hub over the centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement stood in a defensible position on a limestone hill. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 records it as Suindune, believed to derive from the Old English words for swine and hill, or possibly from Sweyn's hill, Sweyn being a Scandinavian name.</p><p>The town remained small until the Great Western Railway chose it for the repair and maintenance of locomotives. Isambard Kingdom Brunel's Swindon Works was built for this purpose, and the company built a small railway village to house its workers. The station opened in 1842 as Swindon Junction, and until 1895 every train stopped for at least ten minutes to change locomotives, so that the station had the first recorded railway refreshment rooms. The railway also provided a medical fund clinic and hospital for its workers, and a health centre of 1892 with clinics, baths and swimming pools.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> new industries arrived: Vickers-Armstrong made aircraft at Stratton and Plessey produced electrical components at Cheney Manor. By 1960 Plessey was the largest employer in the town, with a mostly female workforce. A local council, the Borough of Thamesdown, was created in 1974; it was renamed the Borough of Swindon in 1997 and became a unitary authority on 1 April 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>At the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the built-up area had a population of 224,942, the largest settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>. The Grade II listed Railway Village survives, and the former works house the STEAM Museum of the Great Western Railway and the Swindon Designer Outlet. Other sites include Lydiard House, a Palladian house, and the Richard Jefferies Museum, dedicated to the nature writer. Swindon Town football club won the 1969 League Cup final against Arsenal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swindon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 825 Ecgberht defeated Beornwulf of Mercia at the Battle of Ellendun, near <b>Swindon</b>, a victory that brought Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex under West Saxon rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li></ul></section>
